{
  "response": "# Verdict: REVISE\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe carousel's interactive skeleton is sound \u2014 native `<button>` elements, visible text labels, logical tab order, and no keyboard traps. However, the component is fundamentally **silent to assistive technology**: image changes produce no announcement, the carousel has no landmark or region role, and the active indicator state exists only as a CSS class. A screen reader user can press \"Next\" and receive zero feedback that anything happened. Three of the four expected behaviors listed in the spec (region labeling, live-region announcement, position communication) are absent.\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore reading the code, I predicted the following for a carousel component:\n\n| # | Prediction | Confirmed? |\n|---|-----------|------------|\n| 1 | Missing `aria-live` region for image changes | \u2705 Confirmed |\n| 2 | Missing `role=\"region\"` + `aria-label` on container | \u2705 Confirmed |\n| 3 | Indicator buttons lack programmatic state (`aria-current` / `aria-pressed`) | \u2705 Confirmed |\n| 4 | No \"Image X of Y\" position announcement | \u2705 Confirmed |\n| 5 | Auto-advance without pause control | N/A \u2014 no auto-advance in this implementation |\n\nAll five predictions matched. No surprises. The component implements roughly 40% of the expected carousel accessibility pattern.\n\n---\n\n## Critical Findings (blocks access)\n\n### 1. No `aria-live` region \u2014 image changes are invisible to screen reader users\n\n**Evidence:** `BuggyCarousel.jsx`, lines 1\u201342 (entire component). The `goToPrevious` / `goToNext` / indicator `onClick` handlers call `setCurrentIndex()`, which swaps the `<img src>`. No `aria-live` region, no `role=\"status\"`, no `aria-busy` \u2014 nothing announces the change.\n\n**User group:** Screen reader (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\n\n**WCAG / APG:** WCAG 4.1.3 *Status Messages* \u2014 \"Status messages\u2026 shall be presented in a way that can be announced by assistive technology without requiring the user to focus the element.\" The WAI-ARIA APG Carousel guidance (referenced in the ARIA in HTML spec) explicitly calls for a live region that announces the current image and its position.\n\n**Why this matters:** A screen reader user presses \"Next image.\" The DOM updates. The screen reader says nothing. From the user's perspective, the button did nothing. They may press it again, or give up. The primary function of the component \u2014 *viewing a different image* \u2014 is effectively blocked. The alt text is in the DOM, but the user must manually Tab back to the `<img>` element to discover the change, which is an undiscoverable workaround.\n\n**Fix:**\n```jsx\n<div className=\"carousel\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Image carousel\">\n  {/* Live region that announces changes */}\n  <div aria-live=\"polite\" aria-atomic=\"true\" className=\"sr-only\">\n    {images.length > 0 && `Image ${currentIndex + 1} of ${images.length}`}\n  </div>\n\n  <div className=\"carousel-images\">\n    <img\n      src={images[currentIndex]}\n      alt={images[currentIndex]?.alt || `Image ${currentIndex + 1}`}\n    />\n  </div>\n  {/* ...buttons... */}\n</div>\n```\nThe `sr-only` class should be a visually-hidden utility (position absolute, clip, 1px, etc.) so sighted users don't see the announcement text. `aria-atomic=\"true\"` ensures the full message is read, not just the diff.\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Realist check:** Worst case = screen reader user cannot use the carousel at all. No workaround is discoverable. Detection = immediate in any screen reader test. Keep CRITICAL.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: carousel-no-live-region\nfingerprint: a3f7c21e\nsource: BuggyCarousel.jsx (full component, lines 1-42)\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages; WAI-ARIA APG Carousel guidance\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 Level A (4.1.2) \u2014 in scope for Revised Section 508\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=LOW, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: No aria-live, role=\"status\", or aria-busy anywhere in the component. State change via setCurrentIndex() produces no AT announcement.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Focus \"Next image\" button. 2. Press Enter. 3. Observe: no screen reader announcement. 4. Tab to <img>. 5. Observe: alt text has changed, but user had to manually navigate to discover it.\nexpected_behavior: Screen reader announces \"Image 2 of 5\" (or equivalent) immediately after the image changes.\nactual_behavior: No announcement. User must manually Tab to the image element to hear the new alt text.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n## Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n\n### 2. No `role=\"region\"` or landmark \u2014 carousel is invisible to landmark navigation\n\n**Evidence:** `BuggyCarousel.jsx`, line 14: `<div className=\"carousel\">`. No `role`, no `aria-label`, no landmark element.\n\n**User group:** Screen reader (landmark navigation), Cognitive (context)\n\n**WCAG / APG:** WCAG 1.3.1 *Info and Relationships* \u2014 \"Information, structure, and relationships\u2026 can be programmatically determined.\" The WAI-ARIA APG recommends `role=\"region\"` with `aria-label` for carousels so they appear in landmark navigation.\n\n**Why this matters:** A screen reader user navigating by landmarks (a primary navigation strategy) will not encounter this carousel. It's a nameless `<div>` in the DOM. The user hears a sequence of buttons (\"Previous image, button\", \"Next image, button\", \"Go to image 1, button\"\u2026) with no grouping context. They cannot tell these buttons belong to a carousel versus being scattered page controls.\n\n**Fix:**\n```jsx\n<div className=\"carousel\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Product image carousel\">\n```\nUse a descriptive `aria-label` that tells the user *what* the carousel contains, not just \"carousel.\"\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Realist check:** Workaround exists (Tab navigation reaches the buttons), but the user loses the ability to find the carousel via landmarks and loses grouping context. Keep MAJOR.\n\n### 3. Indicator buttons have no programmatic state \u2014 active image is invisible to AT\n\n**Evidence:** `BuggyCarousel.jsx`, lines 33\u201339:\n```jsx\n<button\n  key={idx}\n  onClick={() => setCurrentIndex(idx)}\n  className={idx === currentIndex ? 'active' : ''}\n  aria-label={`Go to image ${idx + 1}`}\n>\n```\nThe active state is communicated solely via the CSS class `active`. No `aria-current`, no `aria-pressed`, no `aria-selected`.\n\n**User group:** Screen reader, Cognitive\n\n**WCAG / APG:** WCAG 4.1.2 *Name, Role, Value* \u2014 \"For all user interface components (including but not limited to: form elements, links and components generated by scripts), name, role, and value can be programmatically determined.\" The \"value\" here is *which image is currently displayed*.\n\n**Why this matters:** A screen reader user hears \"Go to image 1, button\" / \"Go to image 2, button\" / \"Go to image 3, button\" with no indication which one is active. They cannot determine the current position from the indicators. They might press \"Go to image 3\" not knowing they're already on image 3, or miss that they're on image 1.\n\n**Fix:**\n```jsx\n<button\n  key={idx}\n  onClick={() => setCurrentIndex(idx)}\n  className={idx === currentIndex ? 'active' : ''}\n  aria-label={`Go to image ${idx + 1}`}\n  aria-current={idx === currentIndex ? 'true' : undefined}\n>\n```\nAlternatively, use `aria-pressed={idx === currentIndex}` if you want to frame them as toggle buttons. `aria-current=\"true\"` is the more semantically accurate choice for \"this is the current item in a sequence.\"\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Realist check:** The user can still navigate to the image and hear the alt text, but the indicators \u2014 the primary position indicator \u2014 are silent. Keep MAJOR.\n\n### 4. No \"Image X of Y\" position announcement\n\n**Evidence:** `BuggyCarousel.jsx`, line 20: `alt={`Carousel image ${currentIndex + 1}`}`. The alt text includes the index but not the total count. There is no separate position announcement.\n\n**User group:** Screen reader, Cognitive\n\n**WCAG / APG:** WCAG 4.1.3 *Status Messages*; WAI-ARIA APG Carousel guidance.\n\n**Why this matters:** \"Carousel image 2\" tells the user the index but not the total. The user doesn't know if there are 3 images or 30. Combined with finding #1 (no live region), the user has no way to know their position in the sequence.\n\n**Fix:** Addressed by the live region in finding #1: `Image ${currentIndex + 1} of ${images.length}`. Additionally, consider making the alt text descriptive of the image content rather than a generic index:\n```jsx\nalt={images[currentIndex]?.description || `Image ${currentIndex + 1} of ${images.length}`}\n```\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Realist check:** Partially mitigated by the alt text containing the index, but the total is missing and the announcement is absent. Keep MAJOR (overlaps with finding #1).\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n\n- **Generic alt text:** `alt={`Carousel image ${currentIndex + 1}`}` (line 20) is a placeholder, not a description. In production, each image should have meaningful alt text describing its content. This is a content-level issue, not a code structure issue. **WCAG 1.1.1.** *Fix:* Accept an `alt` or `description` field per image in the `images` array.\n\n- **No `aria-roledescription`:** Adding `aria-roledescription=\"carousel\"` to the region (once `role=\"region\"` is added) helps screen reader users understand the widget type. NVDA and JAWS announce this. **WCAG 4.1.2.** *Fix:* `<div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Product images\" aria-roledescription=\"carousel\">`\n\n- **No keyboard shortcut for arrow keys:** Users may expect `\u2190` / `\u2192` to navigate the carousel. Not required, but the APG carousel guidance suggests it. *Fix:* Add `onKeyDown` handler on the region for `ArrowLeft` / `ArrowRight`.\n\n---\n\n## Enhancements (best practice not met)\n\n- **No `aria-roledescription`** (see Minor above).\n- **No pause/stop mechanism:** This carousel doesn't auto-advance, so WCAG 2.2.2 *Pause, Stop, Hide* is not triggered. If auto-advance is added in the future, a pause control is mandatory.\n- **No `lang` attribute visible:** The component doesn't set `lang`. If the carousel is embedded in a page without `<html lang>`, screen readers may mispronounce content. Verify at the page level.\n- **Indicator container could use `role=\"group\"`:** `<div className=\"carousel-indicators\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Image indicators\">` would help screen reader users understand the group of buttons.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing (gaps, unhandled edge cases)\n\n1. **No `aria-live` region** \u2014 the single most important gap. Image changes are silent to AT. (Finding #1)\n2. **No `role=\"region\"` + `aria-label`** \u2014 carousel is invisible to landmark navigation. (Finding #2)\n3. **No programmatic state on indicators** \u2014 active image not communicated. (Finding #3)\n4. **No position-in-sequence announcement** \u2014 \"Image 2\" without \"of 5.\" (Finding #4)\n5. **No `aria-roledescription`** \u2014 screen reader users don't know this is a carousel.\n6. **No empty-state handling** \u2014 if `images` is an empty array, `images[currentIndex]` is `undefined`, and `<img src={undefined}>` renders a broken image with no error message to AT.\n7. **No `aria-busy`** \u2014 if images load asynchronously (e.g., lazy loading), there's no loading state communicated to AT.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | No live region, no region role, no indicator state, no position announcement |\n| Keyboard-only | **LOW** | All controls are native `<button>`, logical tab order, no traps |\n| Low vision | **MEDIUM** | Indicator state may be color-only (CSS not provided); touch target size unverified |\n| Cognitive | **MEDIUM** | No position context (\"which of how many?\"); no grouping label |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No animations, no auto-advance, no motion |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No audio/video content |\n| Environmental contrast | **MEDIUM** | CSS not provided; cannot verify contrast or forced-colors behavior |\n\n**Screen reader user:** Presses \"Next image.\" Silence. Presses it again. Silence. Tabs to the image. Hears \"Carousel image 2, image.\" Realizes the image changed, but had to manually navigate to discover it. Cannot tell how many images exist. Cannot tell which indicator is active. The carousel is functionally broken.\n\n**Keyboard-only user:** Tabs through Previous \u2192 Next \u2192 1 \u2192 2 \u2192 3. Presses Enter on \"Next.\" Image changes. Sees the new image. Experience is fine. No keyboard traps, no missing controls.\n\n**Low vision user:** Can see the image change. Indicator state depends on CSS (not provided). If the `active` class is color-only (e.g., blue vs. gray), this fails WCAG 1.4.1. Touch targets on the numbered indicators may be small.\n\n**Cognitive user:** Clear button labels. Numbered indicators. Consistent interaction. But no \"Image 2 of 5\" context means the user doesn't know how far through the carousel they are.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**REVISE** \u2014 The component's interactive skeleton is correct (native buttons, visible labels, logical tab order, no keyboard traps). But the accessibility *communication layer* is absent. Three of the four expected behaviors from the spec are missing: no region labeling, no live-region announcement, no position communication. The indicator state is visual-only.\n\nA screen reader user cannot use this carousel in any meaningful way. They can press buttons but receive no feedback about the result. This is not a \"polish\" issue \u2014 it's a broken interaction model for a user category.\n\n**To upgrade to ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS:**\n1. Add `role=\"region\"` + `aria-label` to the container\n2. Add an `aria-live=\"polite\"` region that announces \"Image X of Y\"\n3. Add `aria-current=\"true\"` to the active indicator button\n4. Handle the empty `images` array case\n\n**To upgrade to ACCEPT:**\n5. Add `aria-roledescription=\"carousel\"`\n6. Accept descriptive alt text per image (content-level)\n7. Verify CSS: indicator state is not color-only, touch targets \u2265 24\u00d724px, focus indicators visible\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (unscored)\n\n- **CSS not provided:** The `active` class styling, focus indicators, and touch target sizes cannot be verified. If the active indicator is color-only (e.g., filled vs. unfilled circle with no text difference), this is an additional MAJOR finding (WCAG 1.4.1). Request the CSS or a screenshot.\n- **Auto-advance planned?** If auto-advance is on the roadmap, WCAG 2.2.2 (Pause, Stop, Hide) and 2.5.2 (Pointer Cancellation) become applicable. The current implementation is fine, but the design should account for this.\n- **Images array shape:** The code treats `images` as an array of strings (URLs). If it's an array of objects with `src` and `alt`, the alt text handling changes. Clarify the data contract.\n- **Framework context:** Is this embedded in a page with proper `<html lang>`, `<main>`, and heading structure? The component itself is fine, but the page context matters for landmark navigation.",
